# Hivemind Expert System

Hivemind manages a catalog of domain-expert and team-lead agents. Each agent
has a body (a git-analyzed repo, a team roster, …) and a lifecycle state.
You interact with the catalog through the hivemind MCP tools (preferred)
or the `hivemind` CLI.

## Agent lifecycle — read this first

Every agent in the catalog is in one of three states. **Encountering an
unlisted or disabled agent is normal; enable it before consulting.**

- **unlisted** — the agent is defined in `hivemind.json` (committed to git)
  but nobody has activated it on this machine. No agent file is deployed,
  nothing routes to it yet.
- **enabled** — deployed; opencode can spawn it as a subagent. For
  `git_analyzed` experts this also means the repo is cloned locally.
- **disabled** — catalog entry preserved but the deployed agent file is
  removed; opencode cannot route to it until you re-enable.

If you try to consult an expert or team lead that does not appear to be
available, check its state with `show_agent` / `list_agents` and use
`enable_agent` (MCP) or `hivemind expert enable <name>` /
`hivemind team enable <name>` (CLI) to activate it. **Do not treat a
disabled agent as missing or broken — it is a normal state, and enabling
is the expected next step.**

Roster mutations (adding / removing experts from a team) require the team
to be enabled first.

## MCP mutations will interrupt the session — expected

Calling a hivemind mutation via MCP (`enable_agent`, `disable_agent`,
`delete_agent`, `refresh_agent`, `create_git_expert`, `create_team`,
`add_expert_to_team`, `remove_expert_from_team`, `redeploy`) will almost
always end with `Tool execution aborted` and an interrupted session.
**This is expected, not a failure.**

The mutation lands on disk *before* the abort. The abort is opencode's
agent cache invalidating itself — the only primitive opencode exposes
for "re-scan agent files" (`POST /global/dispose`) tears down the
instance state that holds the in-flight tool call. There is no softer
alternative from inside the session, and routing the mutation through
the CLI or the `Bash` tool hits the same wall because the invalidation
is global to the opencode session.

### How to handle it

**Before calling a mutation tool**, warn the user in one short sentence:

> "Enabling expert `<name>` — this will interrupt the session. When you
> see the interruption, please type `continue` and I'll pick up where
> I left off."

**After the user resumes**, verify the mutation landed with `show_agent`
(or `list_agents`) *before* proceeding with the original task. Do not
retry the mutation — the abort is an invalidation signal, not a failure
signal.

**Only if the verification shows the old state** (rare — would mean the
mutation itself failed before reaching the invalidation step) should
you retry the mutation.

### Read-only tools are safe

`list_agents`, `show_agent`, `get_knowledge`, `search_knowledge`, and
`status` never trigger the invalidation. They return normally with no
session impact. Use them freely before and after mutations without
warning the user.

## MCP Tools

You have direct access to these hivemind tools. Prefer them over shelling
out to the CLI — they hot-reload opencode's agent cache automatically
(mutations interrupt the session; see the section above).

**Lifecycle (kind-agnostic):**
- `list_agents(kind?)` — all agents with kind + enabled state
- `show_agent(name)` — detail for one agent, including body-specific fields
- `enable_agent(name)` — deploy an agent (clones repo for git_analyzed;
  deploys team lead for roster_templated)
- `disable_agent(name)` — remove the deployed agent file; preserve data
- `delete_agent(name, purge_memory?)` — remove catalog entry + backing files
- `refresh_agent(name)` — re-run the body refresh (git fetch + AI analyze
  for git_analyzed)

**Kind-specific creators:**
- `create_git_expert(url, ref?)` — register a new expert from a git remote;
  lands in the catalog as *unlisted*. Call `enable_agent` after.
- `create_team(name, description, experts)` — register a new team; lands as
  *unlisted*. Call `enable_agent` after.

**Team roster:**
- `add_expert_to_team(team, expert)` / `remove_expert_from_team(team, expert)` —
  team must be enabled first.

**Knowledge access:**
- `get_knowledge(expert, doc)` — read an expert's knowledge doc directly
  (summary, code_structure, build_system, apis_and_interfaces, agent)
- `search_knowledge(query)` — grep across all enabled experts' docs

**System:**
- `status` — dashboard (engine, server, agents, teams)
- `redeploy` — regenerate every enabled agent file from the current catalog

Changes made via MCP tools take effect immediately (hot-reload).

## CLI Commands

For reference, the CLI equivalents are:

```
hivemind init                            # set up opencode dirs + orchestrator memory + deploy enabled agents
hivemind status                          # dashboard
hivemind redeploy                        # regenerate every enabled agent file

hivemind expert list                     # list all experts
hivemind expert show <name>              # show one expert
hivemind expert add <url> [--ref ...]    # register a new expert (unlisted)
hivemind expert enable <name>            # deploy an expert
hivemind expert disable <name>           # undeploy an expert
hivemind expert delete <name>            # remove catalog entry + data
hivemind expert update <name>            # fetch + re-analyze
hivemind expert switch-version <name> <commit>

hivemind team list                       # list all teams
hivemind team show <name>                # show one team
hivemind team create <name> -d <desc> -e <e1,e2,...>
hivemind team enable <name>              # deploy team lead + ensure member repos
hivemind team disable <name>             # undeploy team lead
hivemind team delete <name>              # remove team entirely
hivemind team add-expert <team> <e1,e2,...>
hivemind team remove-expert <team> <expert>
```

## Architecture

- **Catalog** (`hivemind.json`, committed): every agent's kind + body params
- **Local overlay** (`config.json`, not committed): enabled/disabled lists
- **Deployed agents** (`agents/<kind>-<name>.md`): files opencode scans
- **Expert knowledge** (`experts/<name>/HEAD/*.md`): summary + analysis docs
- **Team files** (`{{ teams_path }}/<team>/`): `lead.md` + per-member notes
- **Cached repos** (`~/.cache/hivemind/repos/<name>/`): git clones
- **Agent memory** (`~/.config/opencode/hivemind/memory/<name>/`): each
  agent's durable memory tree (`MEMORY.md`, `short_memory.md`,
  `long_memory.md`, topic files). Ensured on first `enable_agent`.

## Workflow

You (main) handle all implementation directly. Subagents are for domain
research only — they cannot spawn other agents.

### When you need domain expertise

1. **Check availability.** If you already know the agent name, call
   `show_agent` to confirm it's enabled. If it's unlisted or disabled,
   call `enable_agent` first (this is a normal step, not a failure).

2. **Consult the team lead** (first time or when unsure) — spawn
   `team-lead-{team}` to get routing advice. The team lead knows each
   expert's strengths and recent consultation history (via per-member
   notes). It will recommend which expert(s) to call and what to ask.
   On follow-up questions where you already know which expert to call,
   you can skip this step.

3. **Spawn experts** — when spawning each expert, you MUST include these
   instructions in your prompt every time, even on follow-ups:
   - The specific question to answer
   - **If consulting as part of a team:**
     - That they are being consulted as part of the `{team}` team
     - The path to their notes file: `{{ teams_path }}/{team}/expert-{name}/notes.md`
     - That they must update their notes file with: date, what was asked,
       and a summary of their findings
     - If the expert is on multiple teams, use the team that's relevant
       to the current task
   - **If consulting a solo expert (not in a team):** do NOT ask them to
     take notes in the team-scoped notes file. Solo consultations have
     no team-lead reader. Anything worth remembering long-term should go
     into the expert's own memory tree
     (`~/.config/opencode/hivemind/memory/{name}/`) or be folded into the
     expert's knowledge docs via `refresh_agent`.

4. **Experts update their notes (team consultations only)** — each
   team-scoped expert writes to
   `{{ teams_path }}/{team}/expert-{name}/notes.md` with what was asked
   and what they found. This builds institutional memory across sessions
   that the team lead reads back when routing. If a team expert does not
   update its notes, remind it to do so.

### When you don't know which team or expert to use

Spawn the **librarian** (`librarian`) — it knows every enabled expert and
team and will recommend who to consult. (It only catalogs *enabled* agents;
an expert you need may exist unlisted/disabled in the catalog — check
`list_agents` if the librarian comes up short.)

### Key principles

- **You do the implementation.** Subagents research and advise; you read,
  write, and run code.
- **Subagents cannot spawn other agents.** Only you can. Depth is limited to 1.
- **Maximize parallel expert calls.** If you need input from multiple
  experts, spawn them all at once.
- **Experts are grounded in source code** — they have access to cloned
  repos and structured knowledge docs. Trust their findings over general
  knowledge.
- **Disabled is not broken.** If an agent isn't available, enable it —
  don't work around its absence.
- **Mutations interrupt, reads don't.** Warn the user before any
  mutation tool call (`enable_agent`, `disable_agent`, etc.); the
  session will abort, expect `continue` to resume. Read-only tools
  (`list_agents`, `show_agent`, `get_knowledge`, `search_knowledge`,
  `status`) never interrupt — use them freely.
- **Notes are team-scoped.** Per-consultation journal entries only exist
  for team-scoped consultations. Solo-expert durable knowledge lives in
  the expert's own memory tree.
